d  from serum that was taken before surgery  at surgery and then post surgery  and here you can see that each of the  three clones are presented  in their specific colors  and you can see that pre-surgery and at  surgery  all three clones are represented at  fairly high levels  whereas right after surgery um  160 days after surgery i should say both  clones a and b  seem to have fallen off and are  undetectable whereas clone c  predominates  however that changes when the patient is  on therapy  and clones a and b take off  clone c is undetectable and the patient  then succumbs to their disease  and the predominant clones are clone a  and p  it is interesting to note that in clone  c is present at the autopsy in plural  fluid  um but i think as i click through and  you can see  each of those variants presented as a  blue box  we have p1 which is pre-surgery p10  at surgery and p16 post surgery you can  see  that what's interesting is the top boxes  are predominantly the clonal variants  and many of those are not even  detectable  post-surgery um whereas they they all  come up  uh at uh the autopsy  it's very uh i think interesting here to  to note that  you know ct dna analysis is rather  challenging and frankly the more shots  on goal that you have that are specific  to that patient  the better outcome you're going to have  um to that end  very important to note here that on day  6 160  the the first time point after surgery  if we were to create a personalized  panel  from the single biopsies  53 of those single biopsies would have  missed  tumor burden post-surgery at the first  time point  and if you think about that that would  essentially be a minimal residual  disease false negative  so i guess our point here is that not  only is representative sampling very  important  when making decisions of those variants  that are coming out of solid tumors  specifically around the clonality of  those variants  when one then uses that sequencing data  potentially to  to make a personal ict dna monitoring  test  the more shots on goal the better and as  we've  demonstrated for this patient you you  will generally and  in all cases so far get more shots on  goal when you homogenize the tumor  versus a single biopsy  so much of that data is  presented in the paper that i mentioned  that was published in  cell reports and i'm happy to point you  in that direction  what i really am interested in  especially for this audience  is our ability to further process this  sample  and then enable it for flow cytometry  interestingly when you put a formula  fixed solid tumor in a blender  it does not become what you think it  would be  uh when we first started this i kind of  thought um well when i make  banana shakes at home i no longer see  the bananas so it must be liquefied  whereas you can see on the image on the  right  a formalin fixed solid tumor gets  chopped up into really small bits  of tumor including single cells  so we have chunks of tumor that are  comprised of many  tens of thousands of cells and single  cells but  we have preserved the unit of  heterogeneity which is the single cell  we can then further  disassociate those fragments of tumor  into single cells  using non-enzymatic methods and  more mechanical dissociation along with  filtration  and depending on the size of the tumor  we can create trillions upon trillions  of cells  both cancer cells immune cells and  stromal cells  i won't belabor this because i know that  the vast majority of people in this  audience are very familiar with flow  cytometry but just suffice it to say  that  what we are doing is again  disassociating these these solid tumors  into single cells  this is non-enzymatic so we're not  degrading the epitopes on the surface of  the cell  and then we are putting these cells  through a fluorocytometer  one important thing i think uh is  of note is that you know around 40 years  ago  in the 80s there were some papers where  people  disassociated tumors fro