de  pretty similar to what we get when we do  that flow from fresh tissue as well  and this is that experiment what we've  done here is we've taken a biopsy  we've split it in half and for one of  those biopsies  we fixed it in formalin overnight  and then disassociated it versus the  other biopsy that was kept fresh  and followed a standard fresh flow  protocol  as you can see in the middle these are  the the images from the flow  you can see uh also very important to  mention that when we do fresh we do have  to do live  discrimination um and it the percentage  of cells that are dead  varies across samples um but we subtract  out the dead cells we go with the fresh  cells  um very important to note but not too  surprising  we don't do that in the fixed because  all of the cells are dead  so when we look at these data on this  from the same biopsy they look  very very similar and then when we look  at these targets across  all samples all biopsies that we've done  most of them  look very very similar almost identical  and the correlation is very very tight  however when you look at the figure on  the on the far right  when you're looking at biomarkers for  macrophages  in the blue these don't necessarily look  similar  um and i think i'll just mention a  couple of things that i  uh that we believe this might be the  case we're we're looking into this a bit  further but  let me just describe what i think is  happening here  it should be noted that the antibodies  that we are using in this test  are designed by ventana for  formalin-fixed  paraffin embedded tissue they work  very well on formalin-fixed cells when  we use them for flow cytometry  that being said it is likely that not  all of these antibodies will work  very well on fresh samples  so you know the the antibodies that  we're using to mark macrophages  maybe just don't work great for fresh  macrophages  so that's one possibility the other  possibility which um  we're very interested in looking at is  whether or not  potentially some of those macrophages  are the dead cells in that live dead  discrimination  one of the reasons why we think this  could be the case and as i mentioned  we're following up is that  you know one of the challenges with the  workflow of  fresh flow cytometry is that you know  the hospital is in one location  and the research might be done at yet a  different location  and cells don't just hang out  and are hunky-dory at four degrees in  specific buffers  you know they change over time and one  of the benefits of fixed flow may very  well be that we are taking  a snapshot in time because the formalin  freezes all of the biomarkers and cells  essentially where they are um it also  definitely helps with the workflow you  can  fix these cells and wait weeks as we do  until we disassociate them  further you can disassociate these fixed  cells and then wait  a few days before you do the experiments  so  we're very interested in how this  workflow can change  you know flow cytometry and the analysis  of cells from solid tumors  but suffice it to say that these data  are very similar to fresh  very reproducible and allow us to do  some really interesting analysis of  these tumors as i'll show you in the  next slides  what can we do with this well we believe  that  this allows us to really analyze the  complexity of solid tumors  in a fundamentally new way really what  we can do is we can look  at really any cell type across an entire  solid tumor  and as you can see we can  look at any number of different cell  types so we can really  very deeply interrogate  immune cells and the types of immune  cells  that have innervated that tumor over  time  we can look at fibroblasts we can look  at the percentage of cancer cells  also we can look at endothelial cells  and what we can do is we can take the  ecology of the solid tumor  and we can describe it in its entirety  across many many many different patients  and then we can compare these patients  to each other  as you can see in this slide  here are a handful of cases that have  been processed from a clinical  feasibility study run at the the where  marsden  with samuel turlich is the pi we  are looking at melanoma ovarian kidney  sarcoma breast this is a pan cancer  study  and it's really looking at the  feasibility of homogenizing solid tumors  as you can see really these these tumors  some of them look similar and some of  them look very very different  and this is really what we're finding  across  all different tumor types that we've  looked at what this  flow cytometry of fixed tissue really  enables us is to really better  appreciate  the diversity at the cellular level of  tumor composition  here are three lung and three colon  cases that i do  specifically want to call out  specifically the stage  two tumors that you'll see both in the  lung at the top and the colon at the  bottom  so these are two tumors same stage  but they are dramatically different in  their cellular composition  for instance um lo2  just the designation that we've given it  only has 11  cancer cells whereas lo7 has  36 the percent of  cytotoxic t cells is also different  and so you can see that even tumors in  the same stage  look very very different from each other  and frankly lo2  that is a stage tumor stage 2 tumor  looks  very similar to lo9 which is  the stage 3 tumor to the left so we just  find these differences to be very very  interesting  the same can be shown down in the bottom  with the colon  again we have very different percentages  of cancer cells  but very strikingly we have in co8  13 cytotoxic t cells whereas in co6 we  only have four percent  um we haven't uh followed up on this  again because all of these patients  and this tissue is taken at surgery all  of this work is  uh prospective so we don't necessarily  have  a deep follow-up and certainly not  five-year follow-up  on any of these cases but it's very  interesting to think what the difference  between 13  cytotoxic t cells and four in the same  stage  of the same tumor type might might  generate relative to  response to immunotherapy  so i'm going to end the talk here by  presenting  um some really interesting data that i  think a lot of you  might be very interested in especially  as  many i believe in the audience are  anatomical pathologists  what we're interested to do here is we  are looking to  enrich for the tumor population using  flow sorting such that we are getting a  more  pure next generation sequencing library  before we sequence these tumors both  saving  sequencing reads and frankly saving  money  so when we sequence bulk populations  we are spending quite a few sequencing  reads on normal cells and if we're  interested in the variance from cancer  frankly those reads are are wasted um  you can see  the if you look into the tcga  and many other repositories of  sequencing data for solid tumors  purity of those tumors is a very  significant challenge  and in many instances very strict  incoming criteria have to be made  such that cases can be sequenced and  this is also true in  clio labs and other labs doing clinical  sequencing  you know it's really important to have a  specific  percentage of those reads be cancer  because anything say under 20 percent  is really going to be at risk when we're  thinking about sequencing of solid  tumors  on the bottom right here you can see  again two different tumors from  this clinical study called whole stuff  run out of the marsden the tumor on the  left has  around 25 cancer cells whereas the tumor  on the right  has uh around 60 cancer cells  so those are two very different outcomes  when we're thinking about sequencing  the the tumor on the left is going to uh  we're going to have to sequence that to  much higher depth to get the equivalent  depth of the tumor on the right  um which will potentially cost time and  maybe even resequencing  so our goal here is to use the same  methodology that enables  low cyt