f different industries  it's based on statistics that were  defined back in the early 1950s  by a mathematician who was in the mining  industry named pierre gee  and essentially what it says is that you  take a spatially segregated sample  you homogenize it and then every sample  you take  will contain the diversity of the  original lot  in every aliquot and you can see that  represented in this cartoon  so essentially this is what we are  attempting to do  and as i hope to convince you through  this talk  in doing so we we generate some very  interesting data and we capture  diversity that is lost in single  biopsies  so to just touch briefly  on how we currently sample solid tumors  i'm very aware that many pathologists  are are probably watching this and  invited to this  to the seminar series so i'm not going  to belabor the point on the left  where we have a couple of diagrams of  the standard samples taken for the tnm  sampling and staging and here you can  see  a lung and a kidney tumor and  you know the the samples are taken many  times are taken  with the tumor and the normal tissue  such that you can look at the interface  for many reasons these samples are  critically important  so that we can stage the tumor  appropriately which  is both prognostic and very important  when we discuss  designing treatment regimens for  patients however  a single sample cannot capture the  diversity of  the entire tumor mass this is very well  demonstrated in the literature both from  from charles swann  samara and and artwork  the the challenge that we have is that  that spatial bias taking a single sample  from one area  leaves you blind to biomarkers that are  present throughout  the rest of the tumor this is also  described as clonal illusion  on the right here you can see data that  is uh  taken from a paper that we just recently  published in cell reports  litchfield at all in in the data that  you see on the right what we did is we  did a survey of 76 samples  of colorectal melanoma and sarcoma  and we looked at what is the average  size  of these tumors and then what is the  median percent of that tumor  that made it into a pcr test  in which three 10 micron sections were  taken  for purification of dna  and as you can see if we just focus on  the far right  you know the the tumors themselves are  about on average the size of a ping pong  ball  whereas the amount of tumor that makes  it into the pcr test is point zero zero  zero five percent and what this does is  this generates the law of small numbers  the law of small numbers is yet another  sort of cognitive bias where samples  that are not representative  tend to generate variable data  especially  when we look at biomarkers or really  any types of data that are present at  low prevalence  so if you have something that is say ten  percent across  a cohort of a thousand patients say  there are many many times though you  will just miss that and you will not  understand really the the total  diversity in that study and this is  really one of the things that we believe  um this process is going to help with  across the board  so this is a cartoon that demonstrates  our workflow  on the top you can see that the current  tissue workflow for surgically resected  solid tumors  surgery happens the tissue goes into  formula and fixation  the samples for the tnm staging are  taken  and those will be ffpe blocks those  blocks  sections are cut and those go through  the standard  diagnostic modalities that exist today  in the clinic  a really important point here is that  the  sample that we are using samples that we  are homogenizing  consist of the residual tumor tissue  that is not incorporated into ffpe  blocks we are not  using ffp blocks we are using the stuff  that gets put in  to trash and today is almost universally  incinerated  with the notable exception of these  samples being  bio-banked by a number of different  companies and institutions  what we are doing is we are having a  surgical pathologists or pathologist  assistants dissect out the rest of the  tumor  and a portion of normal as you can see  on the bottom  we then homogenize these samples  creating  essentially a liquid tumor of that solid  tumor  we then use the normal as the control  when we uh sequence  the tumor and um this has essentially  been  the basis of the work that went into the  the paper that i mentioned  in cell  one point that i would like to point out  again just to repeat  we are not using ffpd blocks many times  i do get questions about this  and we are only using the formalin fixed  tissue  that remains after the tnm staging that  goes back into the bucket and waits  until sign off  so here's just a brief presentation  overview  today i'll be talking briefly about some  ngs downstream of representative  sampling this is  we call this rep seek this is sort of  the foundation of the  paper that i mentioned earlier but then  i want to get into some really  interesting stuff  um where we've we furthered our  methodology  such that we have enabled flow cytometry  of intact cells from fixed  formal and fixed tumors and then i'll  finish with  how we're trying to use the the flow  cytometry and flow sorting  to enrich for tumor cells prior to ngs  which significantly improves the data  output  so first we're going to talk a little  bit about how representative sampling  helps next generation sequencing of  solid tumors  i'm going to present just a brief uh  case report  this is of one very large kidney tumor  there's a clear cell renal cell  carcinoma  in this tumor our colleagues at the  cricket at the marsden  slice this tumor into four very large  slices  otherwise known as bread loafs from  these slices  60 punch biopsies were taken and the  remainder of the tissue  was homogenized i'll also be showing  that blood samples were taken both  pre-surgery and post-surgery  and we used many of the findings from  the homogenous to  better track the disease progression  over time  so the first and most important aspect  of representative sampling  as it relates to ngs is the fact that  when you homogenize tumors you  eliminate clonal illusion as you can see  in the top right the little pie chart  this is  essentially a brief description of what  clonal illusion is  when you take a single sample for  instance either the box  that is across the purple and the gold  slices of the pie or the single box  that's found within the green slice of  pie  both of those samples are biased because  they can't see the other  parts of the pie this is what is  described as clonal illusion  now if you were to homogenize that you  would clearly see that you would  when you end up sampling from that have  about a third purple a third gold  and a third green and this is what we  find in our case study  in this tumor there were two major  subclones  there was a subclone b and c that both  derived from the clone a  it's the parental clone and when we look  at the single biopsies in the middle  what we see is that we have inaccurate  clonality calls  of the variants that we find in the  individual biopsies  all of those 60 biopsies so for instance  we have  clonal variants from clone a that  appeared to be subclonal  but we also have variants in clones b  and c  that are subclonal that appear clonal  and just intuitively when you think  about  subclonal drivers subclonal resistance  mechanisms  seeing that these things are clonal is  not  number one the truth for that tumor and  number two  may very well impact decisions  downstream  whereas on the right when you homogenize  that tumor  and you sequence it following the  homogenization  you get very accurate frequency calls as  you can see  clone a is vastly  clonal whereas clones b and c are all  subclonal  and we've eliminated coronal illusion in  this  in this case  some additional benefits when you use  this method of repseq for this patient  what we did in the middle box is we  looked at  of 