f different industries  it's based on statistics that were  defined back in the early 1950s  by a mathematician who was in the mining  industry named pierre gee  and essentially what it says is that you  take a spatially segregated sample  you homogenize it and then every sample  you take  will contain the diversity of the  original lot  in every aliquot and you can see that  represented in this cartoon  so essentially this is what we are  attempting to do  and as i hope to convince you through  this talk  in doing so we we generate some very  interesting data and we capture  diversity that is lost in single  biopsies  so to just touch briefly  on how we currently sample solid tumors  i'm very aware that many pathologists  are are probably watching this and  invited to this  to the seminar series so i'm not going  to belabor the point on the left  where we have a couple of diagrams of  the standard samples taken for the tnm  sampling and staging and here you can  see  a lung and a kidney tumor and  you know the the samples are taken many  times are taken  with the tumor and the normal tissue  such that you can look at the interface  for many reasons these samples are  critically important  so that we can stage the tumor  appropriately which  is both prognostic and very important  when we discuss  designing treatment regimens for  patients however  a single sample cannot capture the  diversity of  the entire tumor mass this is very well  demonstrated in the literature both from  from charles swann  samara and and artwork  the the challenge that we have is that  that spatial bias taking a single sample  from one area  leaves you blind to biomarkers that are  present throughout  the rest of the tumor this is also  described as clonal illusion  on the right here you can see data that  is uh  taken from a paper that we just recently  published in cell reports  litchfield at all in in the data that  you see on the right what we did is we  did a survey of 76 samples  of colorectal melanoma and sarcoma  and we looked at what is the average  size  of these tumors and then what is the  median percent of that tumor  that made it into a pcr test  in which three 10 micron sections were  taken  for purification of dna  and as you can see if we just focus on  the far right  you know the the tumors themselves are  about on average the size of a ping pong  ball  whereas the amount of tumor that makes  it into the pcr test is point zero zero  zero five percent and what this does is  this generates the law of small numbers  the law of small numbers is yet another  sort of cognitive bias where samples  that are not representative  tend to generate variable data  especially  when we look at biomarkers or really  any types of data that are present at  low prevalence  so if you have something that is say ten  percent across  a cohort of a thousand patients say  there are many many times though you  will just miss that and you will not  understand really the the total  diversity in that study and this is  really one of the things that we believe  um this process is going to help with  across the board  so this is a cartoon that demonstrates  our workflow  on the top you can see that the current  tissue workflow for surgically resected  solid tumors  surgery happens the tissue goes into  formula and fixation  the samples for the tnm staging are  taken  and those will be ffpe blocks those  blocks  sections are cut and those go through  the standard  diagnostic modalities that exist today  in the clinic  a really important point here is that  the  sample that we are using samples that we  are homogenizing  consist of the residual tumor tissue  that is not incorporated into ffpe  blocks we are not  using ffp blocks we are using the stuff  that gets put in  to trash and today is almost universally  incinerated  with the notable exception of these  samples being  bio-banked by a number of different  companies and institutions  what we are doing is we are having a  surgical pathologists or pathologist  assistants dissect out the rest of the  tumor  and a portion of normal as you can see  on the bottom  we then homogenize these samples  creating  essentially a liquid tumor of that solid  tumor  we then use the normal as the control  when we uh sequence  the tumor and um this has essentially  been  the basis of the work that went into the  the paper that i mentioned  in cell  one point that i would like to point out  again just to repeat  we are not using ffpd blocks many times  i do get questions about this  and we are only using the formalin fixed  tissue  that remains after the tnm staging that  goes back into the bucket and waits  until sign off  so here's just a brief presentation  overview  today i'll be talking briefly about some  ngs downstream of representative  sampling this is  we call this rep seek this is sort of  the foundation of the  paper that i mentioned earlier but then  i want to get into some really  interesting stuff  um where we've we furthered our  methodology  such that we have enabled flow cytometry  of intact cells from fixed  formal and fixed tumors and then i'll  finish with  how we're trying to use the the flow  cytometry and flow sorting  to enrich for tumor cells prior to ngs  which significantly improves the data  output  so first we're going to talk a little  bit about how representative sampling  helps next generation sequencing of  solid tumors  i'm going to present just a brief uh  case report  this is of one very large kidney tumor  there's a clear cell renal cell  carcinoma  in this tumor our colleagues at the  cricket at the marsden  slice this tumor into four very large  slices  otherwise known as bread loafs from  these slices  60 punch biopsies were taken and the  remainder of the tissue  was homogenized i'll also be showing  that blood samples were taken both  pre-surgery and post-surgery  and we used many of the findings from  the homogenous to  better track the disease progression  over time  so the first and most important aspect  of representative sampling  as it relates to ngs is the fact that  when you homogenize tumors you  eliminate clonal illusion as you can see  in the top right the little pie chart  this is  essentially a brief description of what  clonal illusion is  when you take a single sample for  instance either the box  that is across the purple and the gold  slices of the pie or the single box  that's found within the green slice of  pie  both of those samples are biased because  they can't see the other  parts of the pie this is what is  described as clonal illusion  now if you were to homogenize that you  would clearly see that you would  when you end up sampling from that have  about a third purple a third gold  and a third green and this is what we  find in our case study  in this tumor there were two major  subclones  there was a subclone b and c that both  derived from the clone a  it's the parental clone and when we look  at the single biopsies in the middle  what we see is that we have inaccurate  clonality calls  of the variants that we find in the  individual biopsies  all of those 60 biopsies so for instance  we have  clonal variants from clone a that  appeared to be subclonal  but we also have variants in clones b  and c  that are subclonal that appear clonal  and just intuitively when you think  about  subclonal drivers subclonal resistance  mechanisms  seeing that these things are clonal is  not  number one the truth for that tumor and  number two  may very well impact decisions  downstream  whereas on the right when you homogenize  that tumor  and you sequence it following the  homogenization  you get very accurate frequency calls as  you can see  clone a is vastly  clonal whereas clones b and c are all  subclonal  and we've eliminated coronal illusion in  this  in this case  some additional benefits when you use  this method of repseq for this patient  what we did in the middle box is we  looked at  of those 60 individual biopsies what was  the reproducibility  of the variant calls and as you can see  the reproducibility is around 76  for any single biopsy and what that  means is any given  biopsy is likely to miss 25 percent  of the variance from any other biopsy  whereas when you homogenize you're  around 97 so it's almost  totally reproducible and this makes  sense given  you know the the mathematical  predictions of representative sampling  another benefit on the right you can see  is the number  of predicted neo antigens that you can  detect  um from the single biopsies on average  you're detecting just above  uh 160 whereas for the the homogeneous  we're detecting  uh upwards of 180. but again i think  more important than the sheer  number of neo-antigens that might be  predicted from the sequencing of these  two different types of samples  it's really important to point out that  each biopsy contains  around 70 17 excuse me percent  of those variants are thought to be  clonal  when in actuality they are subclonal and  again  um you know there's quite a bit of data  out there  suggesting that a clonal neoantigens or  the neon  are the targets that will produce a very  robust  immune response whereas subclonal  and certainly very low prevalent neo  antigens may very well  not and so our point here is that  knowing the clonality and the  appropriate clonality of a variant is  really  important when we start using next  generation sequencing  for downstream decisions for patients  so what we did um with the  initial exome sequencing that i've just  showed you um  apologies i i didn't mean to mention  that all the the data that you've seen  so far  from exome sequencing what we did is we  then took  all of the variants it was around 76  variants that we detected in this  patient  from all samples and we pulled them  together to create a personalized  targeted panel for this specific patient  we then turned that into a ct dna test  and we we sequenced dna purified  from serum that was taken before surgery  at surgery and then post surgery  and here you can see that each of the  three clones are presented  in their specific colors  and you can see that pre-surgery and at  surgery  all three clones are represented at  fairly high levels  whereas right after surgery um  160 days after surgery i should say both  clones a and b  seem to have fallen off and are  undetectable whereas clone c  predominates  however that changes when the patient is  on therapy  and clones a and b take off  clone c is undetectable and the patient  then succumbs to their disease  and the predominant clones are clone a  and p  it is interesting to note that in clone  c is present at the autopsy in plural  fluid  um but i think as i click through and  you can see  each of those variants presented as a  blue box  we have p1 which is pre-surgery p10  at surgery and p16 post surgery you can  see  that what's interesting is the top boxes  are predominantly the clonal variants  and many of those are not even  detectable  post-surgery um whereas they they all  come up  uh at uh the autopsy  it's very uh i think interesting here to  to note that  you know ct dna analysis is rather  challenging and frankly the more shots  on goal that you have that are specific  to that patient  the better outcome you're going to have  um to that end  very important to note here that on day  6 160  the the first time point after surgery  if we were to create a personalized  panel  from the single biopsies  53 of those single biopsies would have  missed  tumor burden post-surgery at the first  time point  and if you think about that that would  essentially be a minimal residual  disease false negative  so i guess our point here is that not  only is representative sampling very  important  when making decisions of those variants  that are coming out of solid tumors  specifically around the clonality of  those variants  when one then uses that sequencing data  potentially to  to make a personal ict dna monitoring  test  the more shots on goal the better and as  we've  demonstrated for this patient you you  will generally and  in all cases so far get more shots on  goal when you homogenize the tumor  versus a single biopsy  so much of that data is  presented in the paper that i mentioned  that was published in  cell reports and i'm happy to point you  in that direction  what i really am interested in  especially for this audience  is our ability to further process this  sample  and then enable it for flow cytometry  interestingly when you put a formula  fixed solid tumor in a blender  it does not become what you think it  would be  uh when we first started this i kind of  thought um well when i make  banana shakes at home i no longer see  the bananas so it must be liquefied  whereas you can see on the image on the  right  a formalin fixed solid tumor gets  chopped up into really small bits  of tumor including single cells  so we have chunks of tumor that are  comprised of many  tens of thousands of cells and single  cells but  we have preserved the unit of  heterogeneity which is the single cell  we can then further  disassociate those fragments of tumor  into single cells  using non-enzymatic methods and  more mechanical dissociation along with  filtration  and depending on the size of the tumor  we can create trillions upon trillions  of cells  both cancer cells immune cells and  stromal cells  i won't belabor this because i know that  the vast majority of people in this  audience are very familiar with flow  cytometry but just suffice it to say  that  what we are doing is again  disassociating these these solid tumors  into single cells  this is non-enzymatic so we're not  degrading the epitopes on the surface of  the cell  and then we are putting these cells  through a fluorocytometer  one important thing i think uh is  of note is that you know around 40 years  ago  in the 80s there were some papers where  people  disassociated tumors from formlin fixed  paraffin embedded samples and isolated  nuclei  which we have also done however the  formula and fixation  we find really damages the cell  membranes and creates very fragile cells  that are really not amenable to flow  cytometry which is  why i think we don't do today flow  cytometry on formula fixed cells  at least of intact cells and so i hope  to convince you in the  in the coming slides that um this method  is very robust  it's very similar to fresh and i think  has  some really interesting outputs that i  believe the  viewers of the seminar will really  really appreciate  so fixed flow is kind of what we're  calling this fixed flow is very  reproducible  as you can see in the cartoon on the  left we take a solid tumor  we homogenize it we then disassociate it  further and filtrate it  so that we create a single cell  suspension and then we stain  in three different experiments for the  same biomarker and then we look at the  variance of that  as you can see on the figure on the  right this is the reproducibility of  fixed flow  looking at two biomarkers cd3 and cd8  the coefficient of variance  is incredibly low across 30 different  patient cases  these tumor types include breast colon  kidney and lung  and if you look on the the figure in the  inset  with the colorful dots what we've done  here is  is essentially looked at the biological  relevance of the data  making sure that we're not wildly off as  cd8 is a component of cd3 positive cells  we certainly don't want to see a bunch  of cda positive cells and very few  cd83 positive cells and what you can see  here is that  the data that we're collecting here  looks very similar to  what data should like when you look at  cd8 and cd3  in some instances we have more cd3 than  we have cd8 as you can see  the two blue dots on the bottom but  suffice it to say that  the data that we're producing across  four different tumor types  is biologically uh reasonable  and as i'll show you in the next slide  pretty similar to what we get when we do  that flow from fresh tissue as well  and this is that experiment what we've  done here is we've taken a biopsy  we've split it in half and for one of  those biopsies  we fixed it in formalin overnight  and then disassociated it versus the  other biopsy that was kept fresh  and followed a standard fresh flow  protocol  as you can see in the middle these are  the the images from the flow  you can see uh also very important to  mention that when we do fresh we do have  to do live  discrimination um and it the percentage  of cells that are dead  varies across samples um but we subtract  out the dead cells we go with the fresh  cells  um very important to note but not too  surprising  we don't do that in the fixed because  all of the cells are dead  so when we look at these data on this  from the same biopsy they look  very very similar and then when we look  at these targets across  all samples all biopsies that we've done  most of them  look very very similar almost identical  and the correlation is very very tight  however when you look at the figure on  the on the far right  when you're looking at biomarkers for  macrophages  in the blue these don't necessarily look  similar  um and i think i'll just mention a  couple of things that i  uh that we believe this might be the  case we're we're looking into this a bit  further but  let me just describe what i think is  happening here  it should be noted that the antibodies  that we are using in this test  are designed by ventana for  formalin-fixed  paraffin embedded tissue they work  very well on formalin-fixed cells when  we use them for flow cytometry  that being said it is likely that not  all of these antibodies will work  very well on fresh samples  so you know the the antibodies that  we're using to mark macrophages  maybe just don't work great for fresh  macrophages  so that's one possibility the other  possibility which um  we're very interested in looking at is  whether or not  potentially some of those macrophages  are the dead cells in that live dead  discrimination  one of the reasons why we think this  could be the case and as i mentioned  we're following up is that  you know one of the challenges with the  workflow of  fresh flow cytometry is that you know  the hospital is in one location  and the research might be done at yet a  different location  and cells don't just hang out  and are hunky-dory at four degrees in  specific buffers  you know they change over time and one  of the benefits of fixed flow may very  well be that we are taking  a snapshot in time because the formalin  freezes all of the biomarkers and cells  essentially where they are um it also  definitely helps with the workflow you  can  fix these cells and wait weeks as we do  until we disassociate them  further you can disassociate these fixed  cells and then wait  a few days before you do the experiments  so  we're very interested in how this  workflow can change  you know flow cytometry and the analysis  of cells from solid tumors  but suffice it to say that these data  are very similar to fresh  very reproducible and allow us to do  some really interesting analysis of  these tumors as i'll show you in the  next slides  what can we do with this well we believe  that  this allows us to really analyze the  complexity of solid tumors  in a fundamentally new way really what  we can do is we can look  at really any cell type across an entire  solid tumor  and as you can see we can  look at any number of different cell  types so we can really  very deeply interrogate  immune cells and the types of immune  cells  that have innervated that tumor over  time  we can look at fibroblasts we can look  at the percentage of cancer cells  also we can look at endothelial cells  and what we can do is we can take the  ecology of the solid tumor  and we can describe it in its entirety  across many many many different patients  and then we can compare these patients  to each other  as you can see in this slide  here are a handful of cases that have  been processed from a clinical  feasibility study run at the the where  marsden  with samuel turlich is the pi we  are looking at melanoma ovarian kidney  sarcoma breast this is a pan cancer  study  and it's really looking at the  feasibility of homogenizing solid tumors  as you can see really these these tumors  some of them look similar and some of  them look very very different  and this is really what we're finding  across  all different tumor types that we've  looked at what this  flow cytometry of fixed tissue really  enables us is to really better  appreciate  the diversity at the cellular level of  tumor composition  here are three lung and three colon  cases that i do  specifically want to call out  specifically the stage  two tumors that you'll see both in the  lung at the top and the colon at the  bottom  so these are two tumors same stage  but they are dramatically different in  their cellular composition  for instance um lo2  just the designation that we've given it  only has 11  cancer cells whereas lo7 has  36 the percent of  cytotoxic t cells is also different  and so you can see that even tumors in  the same stage  look very very different from each other  and frankly lo2  that is a stage tumor stage 2 tumor  looks  very similar to lo9 which is  the stage 3 tumor to the left so we just  find these differences to be very very  interesting  the same can be shown down in the bottom  with the colon  again we have very different percentages  of cancer cells  but very strikingly we have in co8  13 cytotoxic t cells whereas in co6 we  only have four percent  um we haven't uh followed up on this  again because all of these patients  and this tissue is taken at surgery all  of this work is  uh prospective so we don't necessarily  have  a deep follow-up and certainly not  five-year follow-up  on any of these cases but it's very  interesting to think what the difference  between 13  cytotoxic t cells and four in the same  stage  of the same tumor type might might  generate relative to  response to immunotherapy  so i'm going to end the talk here by  presenting  um some really interesting data that i  think a lot of you  might be very interested in especially  as  many i believe in the audience are  anatomical pathologists  what we're interested to do here is we  are looking to  enrich for the tumor population using  flow sorting such that we are getting a  more  pure next generation sequencing library  before we sequence these tumors both  saving  sequencing reads and frankly saving  money  so when we sequence bulk populations  we are spending quite a few sequencing  reads on normal cells and if we're  interested in the variance from cancer  frankly those reads are are wasted um  you can see  the if you look into the tcga  and many other repositories of  sequencing data for solid tumors  purity of those tumors is a very  significant challenge  and in many instances very strict  incoming criteria have to be made  such that cases can be sequenced and  this is also true in  clio labs and other labs doing clinical  sequencing  you know it's really important to have a  specific  percentage of those reads be cancer  because anything say under 20 percent  is really going to be at risk when we're  thinking about sequencing of solid  tumors  on the bottom right here you can see  again two different tumors from  this clinical study called whole stuff  run out of the marsden the tumor on the  left has  around 25 cancer cells whereas the tumor  on the right  has uh around 60 cancer cells  so those are two very different outcomes  when we're thinking about sequencing  the the tumor on the left is going to uh  we're going to have to sequence that to  much higher depth to get the equivalent  depth of the tumor on the right  um which will potentially cost time and  maybe even resequencing  so our goal here is to use the same  methodology that enables  low cytometry of these fixed samples and  then  sort these tumors to enrich for cancer  cells  prior to creating our ngs library  so you can see here in in this series of  figures  the benefit of doing this relative to to  sequencing depth and  output in the bottom left  this is a colon tumor that was sorted  based on cytokine 818  and you can see here that on the bottom  left 44 percent of that tumor was ck818  positive we then sequenced  both the homogeneous the mixed  population of cells  and the enriched tumor and you can see  in the top right  that the flow enriched sample which is  the cells that were sorted based on  cytokine 818  had almost a doubling in tumor purity  even at the same sequencing depth and so  our tumor cell coverage  is more than doubled which then  generates on the far right  65 additional variants that we find  versus in the homogenous there were only  five variants we did not find in the  flow enriched  and those five variants hovered around  five percent which was our cut-off  more importantly those 65 additional  variants  better enabled us to understand  the complexity at the level of the  phylogenetic tree  of this specific case so you can see in  the homogeneous when we look at it we  only really see two subclones very  similar to the kidney tumor that i  i showed you as our original test case  whereas when we sort this tumor we see  that there are  three additional clones that weren't  even found in that module  moreover we see that one of those clones  has  what we described as a leaf on it so an  even further  uh subclone that is from that population  on the far left and orange  so not only does enriching for  cancer cells can generate higher tumor  cell coverage  but it also can enable us to better  understand the evolutionary trajectory  of solid tumors  so we have done this across samples from  our clinical  feasibility study here we have breast  ovarian gist and kidney on the left  you can see that in all four cases we  consistently have enrichment  from the homogeneity and on the right  every time we do enrich we detect more  variants  in some instances we detect more than  double the number of variants in the  homogeneity  and in other cases we find around you  know 20 to  30 more variants suffice it to say that  these data are incredibly helpful when  we're trying to look at what i mentioned  earlier which is the  the clonal solutions of these tumors  relative to the potential evolutionary  trajectories  so when we think about enrichment  relative to clinical findings  i do want to point out one specific case  that one of the kidney cases that i just  showed you  so in this case we had three ffpe blocks  and when we do exome sequencing  what we see is that um in  ffpe block 1 we have a clonal  vhl mutation and we can see a low  prevalence baf1 mutation  however an ffp block number two we only  see the vhl clonal mutation and we miss  the bat  one and in ffpe3 the bap1  g194a mutation appears to be clonal  that is clonal illusion whereas it's  actually subclone  however in the the sordid repseq not  only do we  totally define that the vhl is a clonal  variant  we better define the actual prevalence  of the first pap one  mutation and we see a second  subclonal backbone mutation so we have a  convergence on this bap1  mutation through multiple mechanisms  and we describe that as convergent  evolution and this is important  um and if we look at this relative to  the outcome  of patients with these molecular  features and this is  data taken from uh samurai turlich at  all  it was a cell paper back in 2018  a vhl only mutation those patients had a  very very positive  outcome whereas when you have a back one  mutation  that accompanies a clonal vhl mutation  those patients do not have  as good of an outcome and so really what  we're showing here is that not only are  we  able to with representative sampling  followed up by  next generation sequencing capable of  defining  the true mutant allele 